feat(dashboard): draw the run timeline as a rail, and strip the header (RM-141)

The run detail had the approved design's tabs and serif heading and none of
its body. This is the body: a facts strip, a rail, and the exceptions given
somewhere to stand out from.

Driven by six new test-catalog rows, written and watched failing first
(30 assertions across four files), plus one more that closes the hole the
last round left open.

FE-122  the facts are one bordered strip of labelled columns -- agent, task,
        tool calls, commits, then registered, ended, chain position, run --
        and the full SPIFFE ID sits under the heading in mono with no label.
        The uppercase field label is now ONE class string, shared with the
        runs view's filter labels and every table's column headers, so the
        three cannot be restyled apart. Nothing doc 06 3.3 names was dropped:
        the credential expiry history became a real table drawn from the
        shared table module, and FE-082's assertions are re-asserted against
        the new treatment.

FE-123  the timeline is a rail -- a marker per row, a connector between
        markers, stopping at the last so no stroke claims a chain beyond this
        response. An ordinary event now carries no ground and no border at
        all, which is what gives the amber band, the red fill and the commit
        card something to step away from (doc 06 P3). The rail itself is
        neutral in every case; a node is not a verdict, so the exception is
        carried by the marker's SHAPE.

FE-124  a folded run of tool calls is a disclosure pill carrying the exact
        count and the exact span of chain positions it hides.

FE-125  `source:` moved rather than vanished. doc 02 3 gives eleven of the
        twelve event types one legal writer, so the label restated the event
        type on every row -- 1220 of them on a real run -- and that is what
        made the repaired one unreadable. It is now on the rail when the type
        permits more than one writer (`commit_recorded` alone, which is
        exactly the repair case) or when the writer is one the type does not
        permit at all, and inside the event's own evidence disclosure
        otherwise. Repaired history is untouched: label, badge, amber ground
        and sentence, all still on the rail. FE-012 passes unchanged.

FE-126  `run_expired` reads as degraded. doc 06 3.2's "an agent died
        unretired" is a degradation, so it takes the amber band and words of
        its own -- distinct from an expired commit intent, which is a promise
        nobody kept.

FE-127  the activity log's mismatch count is a red chip on the tab row. It is
        the one condition that can only be found behind the tab the page does
        not open with, so the read was lifted into the view and the chip
        renders outside both panels -- the same argument FE-113 makes about
        the banner. The altered mark in that panel is now actually red: it
        named a utility no token backs and compiled to nothing.

FE-128 is the new gate. The suite was fully green while the page was visibly
broken, for two reasons this closes. The fixtures were short -- `run-7f3a2c`
fits anywhere -- so no baseline had ever rendered an identifier at the width
the product issues; they are full length now. And nothing measured layout, so
this walks the six views at 1280px and 720px and refuses any box whose content
is wider than it is. It found two real defects outside this view, both fixed:
the identifier chip could not shrink below its content, and the repo and
agent-type tables had no scroll shell and took the page sideways with them.
Watched failing: stripping `break-all` from `identifierText` fails 5 of its 12.

Also here, from a browser measurement the visual fixtures could not reach: the
`break-all` and `min-w-0` overflow fix on the verify page's check cards.

Regenerated `panel-mismatch` light and dark, darwin and linux -- that fixture's
identifiers wrap differently now that they are allowed to wrap. No other
baseline moved.

The timeline panel lost its raised ground and sits on the page ground, which
puts muted ink on that surface for the first time; the pair is declared in
contrast-pairs.txt and measured in both modes.

